         information too.
         ============================================================ -->

    <h2>The question</h2>
    <p>
      In New York City, where do high population density and densely built-up areas go together because of housing, jobs and transportation accessibility?
    </p>

    <h2>The data</h2>
    <p>
      I combined the Sentinel-2 annual median true-colour satellite layer with the WorldPop 2020 population-density layer in the Site Scanner. 
      The satellite imagery shows the physical landscapes, while the WorldPop layer estimates the number of people living in each approximately 100-metre grid cell. 
      The population data layer only represents the estimated residential population instead of showing neither daytime population nor daytime population. To understand the question, I need to obtain the total population of the place. 
    </p>
    <p>
      Write the proxy sentence here, once per indicator:
      <em>I am using total residential population to stand for total population, and it might fail because residential population is just one category of total population.</em>
    </p>

    <h2>The method</h2>
    <p>
      First, I selected New York in the Site Scanner and used “Satellite (true colour)” as the underlying layer. 
      Secondly, I then placed “People (population density)” on top and set its opacity to 0.75. 
      Thirdly, I set map’s scale bar to approximately five kilometres, so that population patterns across New York City and surrounding areas could be compared.
    </p>

